description:
Found by the s525q binder dual-run, perl t/op/universal.t:186 (`package HumanTie; ... tie my($x), "HumanTie"; ::ok $x->isa("Human")`) and t/op/taint.t:1763 (`tie my ($foo), Tie => $bar;`): `_binding_at` (via _requalify_region < _requalify_switched_regions < _requalify_block_globals_after_pkg_switch) answers undef for the DECLARATOR token `$x`; the binder answers `my $x` (the token is its own declaration).

Probe (scratch/s525q/probes/F1.pl in the s525q worktree):

    sub run {
        my $pre = 1;
        package HumanTie;
        sub TIESCALAR { bless {} }
        sub FETCH { "Human" }
        tie my($x), "HumanTie";
        print "x=$x pre=$pre\n";
        print defined(tied $HumanTie::x) ? "global tied\n" : "global untied\n";
    }
    run();

perl 5.40.3:  x=Human pre=1 / global untied
base (main 81ef05e5) ./runpcl:  x=Human pre=1 / global tied   -- SILENT WRONG: the emission is `(p-tie HumanTie::$x "HumanTie")` and every use reads HumanTie::$x; the `my` is gone and the package global is tied instead.
s525q tree: identical to base (inert).
Control F2.pl (`print f(my ($x) = (5))` in the same shape) is right, so the shape is the `tie my(...)` spelling (pass `_normalize_tie_my` runs early -- check what it leaves for the requalifier's `_stmt_binding` / `_symbol_is_declarator`).

Fix belongs to 1c (requalifier port).  NOT fixed in s525q (inert batch).


